{"id": "62fca5cc-100c-44b9-8a8a-c842de91a5a4", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I need a full report on long-term Bedrock API keys, which are IAM service-specific credentials tied to IAM users — pulling this means iterating through every user across all accounts, which is token-heavy so I should delegate to a subagent rather than doing it myself. I can't delete these keys even if asked, and since I'm read-only here, I'll have the subagent gather the data first and decide afterward whether a report or artifact makes more sense.

I also want the subagent to check CreateDate, ExpirationDate, Status, attached policies like AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess, and ideally last-used activity via CloudTrail lookups, since this is a comprehensive inventory task rather than an open-ended investigation.

"}]}

{"id": "3546573f-19ec-4147-a474-f378aa91238d", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll inventory the long-term Bedrock API keys in every account associated with this agent space. These are IAM service-specific credentials for `bedrock.amazonaws.com`. For each key I'll collect its status, age, expiry, owning IAM user, permissions and recent usage, and then recommend which to remove. I'm running this in the background because it means scanning every IAM user in every account.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_sVzcKPz5cc350lav6eEsEw", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-api-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Produce a complete, read-only inventory and risk assessment of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key across ALL AWS accounts associated with this agent space (use list_associations / get_associations to enumerate accounts; the agent space's own account is 012345678901_1, region us-east-1). Do NOT make any mutating calls.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName 'bedrock.amazonaws.com', attached to IAM users (often named BedrockAPIKey-xxxx, created via the Bedrock console, but they can also be attached to any IAM user). IAM is global, so query IAM per account (use us-east-1). The user wants a full report and a recommendation on which keys to remove. Current time is 2026-11-25T08:20Z.",
  "how_to_enumerate": "For each account: iam list_users (paginate), then iam list_service_specific_credentials with ServiceName='bedrock.amazonaws.com' per user (or without the user filter if that lists all). Record per key: account ID, IAM user name, ServiceSpecificCredentialId, ServiceCredentialAlias, Status (Active/Inactive/Expired), CreateDate, ExpirationDate (or 'never expires'), key age in days. Per owning user also gather: attached managed policies, inline policies, group memberships, whether the user has console login profile / access keys / MFA, user tags, user last-used (PasswordLastUsed), and whether the user appears to be a dedicated BedrockAPIKey-* user or a general human/service user. Flag over-permissive policies (e.g., AdministratorAccess, bedrock:*, *:*, anything beyond AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess/ bedrock:InvokeModel*), and any explicit expiry-less keys.",
  "last_used": "Try to determine last use of each key: IAM get_user / iam get_service_last_accessed_details for the user (bedrock services), and use lookup_cloudtrail_events (reads via the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill) for bedrock-runtime / bedrock events by the IAM user over the last 90 days, plus CreateServiceSpecificCredential / ResetServiceSpecificCredential / UpdateServiceSpecificCredential events to find who created each key and when. State clearly where last-use could not be determined.",
  "criteria": "Classify each key as REMOVE, REVIEW, or KEEP with a one-line reason. Suggested REMOVE signals: Inactive or expired status; no usage in 90+ days; never-expiring keys with no recent use; keys on users with broad/admin permissions; keys on human users with console access; orphaned/unknown owner; age > 90 days with no rotation. REVIEW: used but long-lived/never-expiring or overly broad. KEEP: recently used, scoped to least privilege, with an expiry. Also report accounts you could not access or errors encountered, and total counts per account and per classification. Return a concise structured result: summary counts, a table of all keys (one row each) with the fields above, and the recommendations."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit long-term Bedrock API keys across associated accounts"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "a52b8861-d94f-40fa-af7d-c00411d2dd53", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm trying to figure out how bedrock API key usage would show up under CloudTrail, since InvokeModel is a data event not captured in LookupEvents, and checking whether ListFoundationModels management events reveal anything under the llmops-lf-apikey-user identity — so far nothing turns up there, so I should also check the user's last-used timestamp.

Checking via get_user shows no PasswordLastUsed, which seems sufficient, but I'll do one more lookup for ResourceName to rule out update or reset events.

"}], "parent_id": "fc289132-529d-4193-9983-53f60ba26350"}

